{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_0","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc 'cat docs/research/2026-11-14-use-acceptance/figure-fields-2/reader-packet-2-part3.json'","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_0","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc 'cat docs/research/2026-11-14-use-acceptance/figure-fields-2/reader-packet-2-part3.json'","aggregated_output":"{\n \"articles\": [\n  {\n   \"article\": 1,\n   \"title\": \"Nvidia Gains as Apple Pulls AI Off the Meter\",\n   \"publisher\": \"gurufocus.com\",\n   \"publication_date\": \"2026-09-22\",\n   \"text\": \"Nvidia ( NASDAQ:NVDA ), the AI-accelerator and data-center platform leader, faced a fresh edge-computing test as Apple ( NASDAQ:AAPL ) pushed its new Macs as machines capable of running heavyweight AI privately instead of constantly calling the cloud. Nvidia shares gained approximately 0.7% to $229.08, suggesting investors were hardly treating Apple's move as an immediate threat to the GPU giant's core data-center engine.\\nWarning! GuruFocus has detected 3 Warning Sign with MSFT.\\nApple showed four Mac Studios working together to run a trillion-parameter model, using shared memory across the machines to tackle workloads once associated almost entirely with expensive server infrastructure. That is an eye-catching demonstration, but the scale gap remains enormous: Nvidia's latest quarter generated $89 billion of data-center revenue out of $96.2 billion overall, meaning roughly 92.5% of sales came from the infrastructure businesses powering large-scale AI. The GuruFocus picture reinforces that underlying strength, with Nvidia carrying a GF Score of 95/100 and particularly strong readings for profitability, growth and financial strength, while GF Value remains the noticeably weaker part of the profile.\\nThe bigger question is not whether a handful of Macs can run a giant model. They clearly can. The question is how much inference, coding and repetitive enterprise AI work permanently shifts from cloud infrastructure onto local machines. Apple could chip away at some private and predictable workloads, especially where data security and recurring token costs matter. But hyperscale training, massive networking demands and unpredictable peak workloads still play directly into Nvidia's territory. For Nvidia investors, the real risk starts only if local AI becomes a structural substitute for cloud inference rather than another layer of the computing stack.\"\n  },\n  {\n   \"article\": 2,\n   \"title\": \"Nvidia stock is approaching its cheapest valuation in a decade despite booming profits\",\n   \"publisher\": \"qz.com\",\n   \"publication_date\": \"2026-09-22\",\n   \"text\": \"Nvidia stock is trading at less than 17 times forward earnings, half the multiple it commanded in 2025, even as revenue and net income are expected to surge\\nNvidia $NVDA stock is trading near its lowest valuation in more than a decade, according to Bloomberg , even as the chipmaker's revenue and net income are each expected to grow by roughly 90% or more in its current fiscal year.\\nAt under 17 times forward earnings, the stock's multiple has been cut in half compared with 2025 — a period when Nvidia 's expansion was actually slower — and has fallen sharply from above 25 times expected profits just this past May, according to Bloomberg.\\n\\\"The stock has de-rated pretty significantly, which suggests a healthy dose of skepticism that the company's current earnings power is sustainable,\\\" Eli Horton, a TCW senior portfolio manager overseeing thematic and durable growth equities, told Bloomberg. \\\"The stock's performance is surprising, given the backdrop of incredible fundamentals, but it tells you the market is expecting less than what the consensus is currently estimating.\\\"\\nOne drag on the stock's valuation is a squeeze on profitability. Nvidia 's gross margin came in at 75% last quarter but analyst estimates compiled by Bloomberg show it sliding under 72% by the fourth quarter, with a recovery expected thereafter. Rising costs for key components such as memory chips are a central factor.\\nCompetition is another concern. David Russell, global head of market strategy at TradeStation, pointed to the growing threat of customers building their own silicon, noting that Meta $META Platforms and Alphabet $GOOGL are among the major Nvidia buyers that have moved to design AI chips internally — a shift he believes will gradually erode Nvidia 's pricing power and market share. \\\"Multiples expand when companies are well positioned with potential to get better, and Nvidia doesn't offer that,\\\" Russell said.\\nNvidia stock has gained 22% so far in 2026, putting it second among the Magnificent Seven behind Apple $AAPL 's 25% advance — yet that performance looks modest against the broader chip sector. The Philadelphia Stock Exchange Semiconductor Index has surged nearly 76%, with Intel $INTC and Advanced Micro Devices both more than tripling; Nvidia sits near the bottom of the index's leaderboard, even as the index itself carries a valuation of 20 times forward earnings.\\nNvidia Chief Executive Officer Jensen Huang has pushed back on the market's assessment, calling the company \\\"the world's first and only growth value stock\\\" and describing Nvidia as \\\"incredibly misunderstood\\\" at a Goldman Sachs $GS technology conference earlier this month.\\nThe valuation discount follows a stretch of strong results. Nvidia stock climbed roughly 7% after its second-quarter earnings , when revenue more than doubled and the company projected 70% revenue growth in fiscal 2028 — well above the 45% analysts had expected at the time.\\nHorton said the current multiple looks favorable given that neither a pullback in AI infrastructure spending nor a major regulatory intervention appears imminent. \\\"This seems like a very favorable multiple to have as an entry point,\\\" he said.\"\n  },\n  {\n   \"article\": 3,\n   \"title\": \"Will Nvidia be allowed to sell more chips to China? This former Trump economic adviser has a hot take.\",\n   \"publisher\": \"finance.yahoo.com\",\n   \"publication_date\": \"2026-09-23\",\n   \"text\": \"Will Nvidia be allowed to sell more chips to China? This former Trump economic adviser has a hot take.\\nWhen Nvidia ( NVDA ) CEO Jensen Huang arrives at the state dinner on Thursday between President Trump and Chinese President Xi Jinping, you better believe he'll be hoping to get a sense of when — and to what extent — his business may resume in China.\\nBoth sides coming to a deal on this front won't be easy, given the politics still at play.\\n\\\"My personal opinion is that we need to have as much competitive advantage here in the United States as we can, because we've got competitive disadvantages against China,\\\" said Gary Cohn, IBM ( IBM ) vice chair and former economic adviser to President Trump in his first term, on Yahoo Finance's Sozzi Unleashed . \\\"You know, they don't have environmental issues. They don't have a cost of capital. They can build data centers wherever they want. The citizens can't stand up and say not in my backyard.\\\"\\nCohn added, \\\"To the extent that the Chinese have all these other significant advantages, the one advantage we have is we've got the chips, and we've got the chip manufacturing here. I'd like to keep that advantage as long as we can. \\\"\\nThe Trump administration has allowed Nvidia to ship its H200 AI chips to approved customers in China. The problem for Nvidia: The H200 is already an older-generation product, trailing Nvidia's current Blackwell chips in terms of performance.\\nIn other words, Nvidia is likely leaving billions in sales on the table by being unable to get Blackwell chips into China to feed the country's AI startup scene.\\nChinese authorities, however, have so far restricted shipments of Nvidia's H200 chips.\\nWhile Nvidia hasn't factored any sales from China into its outlook for the year, the stock is still up 21% year to date, powered by the AI boom here in the US.\"\n  },\n  {\n   \"article\": 4,\n   \"title\": \"Nvidia Stock Could Surge by 2028: Here's How Beginners Can Get In Now According to Motley Fool's Price Estimates\",\n   \"publisher\": \"fool.com\",\n   \"publication_date\": \"2026-09-23\",\n   \"text\": \"Even if you're a total novice to the stock market, you've probably heard of Nvidia ( NVDA +2.55% ) .\\nNvidia is now the most valuable company in the world, worth more than $5 trillion. Its chips have formed the backbone of the AI revolution, and the top AI labs and cloud computing platforms rely on them to make AI applications run.\\nIf you were fortunate enough to buy Nvidia a decade ago and hold it, you would have made a huge return as the stock is up more than 14,000% during that time. That means a $1,000 investment in Nvidia would now be worth more than $14,000.\\nThe stock won't do that again over the next decade, but it still looks poised to soar by 2028. Here's how I see it.\\nThere's a lot of jargon in investing, but one of the most important concepts that beginning investors can learn is the price-to-earnings ratio , or P/E ratio. This is a company's price per share divided by its earnings per share.\\nNewbie investors often think that a stock's share price determines whether it is cheap or not. For instance, a $10 stock would be cheap and a $500 stock would be expensive, but the share price alone isn't really meaningful.\\nOwning a share of a stock is a claim on its earnings, so what really matters is how much earnings you get relative to the price of the stock. That's the P/E ratio. The lower it is, the cheaper the stock.\\nNvidia now trades at a P/E of 29, which is slightly higher than the S&P 500's P/E ratio of 25. The S&P 500 , an index that holds 500 of the biggest U.S. stocks, is generally seen as the market average. According to the numbers above, Nvidia is a bit more expensive than the S&P 500. However, the P/E ratio is backward-looking, and stocks are priced based on future earnings or cash flows.\\nBecause of its central position in the AI boom, Nvidia is growing much faster than the S&P 500. Wall Street expects the company to grow revenue by 91% this year and 66% next year. For fiscal 2028, which ends in January of that year, Nvidia is expected to have earnings per share of $15.68.\\nBased on that forecast, the stock trades at 14.6x next year's earnings. or a forward P/E of 14.6. If Nvidia maintains its current P/E ratio and hits that EPS forecast, the stock would double in less than a year and a half.\\nIf you're new to investing and you want to buy Nvidia stock , you'll first need a brokerage account if you don't already have one. You can open one up with a platform like Robinhood , Interactive Brokers , E*TRADE from Morgan Stanley , or Charles Schwab . There are a lot of options for brokers.\\nNvidia is currently trading around $228 a share, so you'll need that much to buy one share. However, many brokerages now offer fractional share purchasing , meaning you can buy a fraction of a share of Nvidia, allowing you to get ownership for less than the price of a share. Once you have an account, you'll need to search for the stock using the ticker, NVDA, and then choose an order type. Using a market order will execute the trade immediately and is the easiest way to buy a heavily traded stock like Nvidia. If you want to set a maximum price you're willing to pay, you can use a limit order.\\nKeep in mind that while Nvidia's future looks promising and the numbers favor continued gains in the stock, there is always risk in stock investing. There is no guarantee that the stock will go up between now and 2028. With any investment, it's a good idea to keep a long-term view and continue to follow the stock. Don't just buy and forget. If things change, you may want to sell.\\nStill, I think the odds are in your favor. As long as the AI boom continues over the next two years, Nvidia looks well-positioned to be a winner.\\nCharles Schwab is an advertising partner of Motley Fool Money. Jeremy Bowman has positions in Nvidia. The Motley Fool has positions in and recommends Interactive Brokers Group and Nvidia. The Motley Fool recommends Charles Schwab and recommends the following options: long January 2027 $43.75 calls on Interactive Brokers Group, short January 2027 $46.25 calls on Interactive Brokers Group, and short September 2026 $95 calls on Charles Schwab. The Motley Fool has a disclosure policy .\\nBy Jeremy Bowman – Sep 23, 2026 at 12:00AM EST\"\n  },\n  {\n   \"article\": 5,\n   \"title\": \"Nvidia’s Last Quarter Was Nearly Perfect. One Number in the Fine Print Wasn’t.\",\n   \"publisher\": \"fool.com\",\n   \"publication_date\": \"2026-09-23\",\n   \"text\": \"Nvidia ( NVDA +2.55% ) recently reported $96.2 billion in revenue for the fiscal second quarter, which was 106% more than in the same period last year. The company also produced a 75% gross margin, and management told investors to expect roughly 70% revenue growth in the next fiscal year.\\nBy most numbers, Nvidia reported a stellar quarter. However, a closer look at the company's balance sheet shows that Nvidia's total assets jumped by more than 50% over the past six months. Here's why that matters, and what I'll be watching closely over the next few quarters of Nvidia's AI-driven growth.\\nNvidia's spectacular quarter, with one caveat\\nIt's difficult to overstate how strong Nvidia's fiscal second quarter was. Revenue and earnings both handily beat expectations, and Nvidia guided to a jaw-dropping $108 billion in revenue for the current quarter, representing 17% sequential growth.\\nAs mentioned earlier, Nvidia's total assets grew rapidly. A big part of that is the company's receivables rising from $38.5 billion in January to $63.1 billion at the end of July.\\nOf course, some growth in receivables is to be expected, given Nvidia's spectacular revenue growth. After all, if you sell more products, you can expect more unpaid invoices at any given time. But one number to note is \\\"days sales outstanding,\\\" or DSO. This is the measure of how long, on average, it takes a company to collect after making a sale. Nvidia's DSO went from 45 to 60 days in one quarter. Customers are taking longer to pay.\\nTo be fair, Nvidia told us why. The company extended payment terms on large orders from certain customers.\\nOn one hand, Nvidia offering of flexible terms to its strongest customers helps them lock in multi-year deals. On the other hand, Nvidia is increasingly helping finance its customers' orders, which raises the question of whether demand appears higher in the revenue numbers than it otherwise would.\\nThis is just the latest in a string of \\\"circular\\\" financial moves. For example, Nvidia agreed to guarantee up to $105 billion of data center lease obligations for OpenAI. It has formed partnerships to raise over $500 billion of outside capital for AI infrastructure. In simple terms, Nvidia is actively helping customers pay for its products.\\nThis is especially worth watching for Nvidia stock investors, given the recent push to slow down the pace of AI development. While a call to slow AI development isn't the same thing as a cut to infrastructure budgets, the DSO number, as well as receivables and inventories, are certainly worth keeping an eye on over the next few quarters.\\nIf the DSO number stabilizes, and receivables and inventories rise in line with Nvidia's revenue, it's a very good sign. On the other hand, if these numbers continue to grow faster than the top line, it could indicate a spending slowdown.\"\n  },\n  {\n   \"article\": 6,\n   \"title\": \"I'm Buying More Nvidia Stock Right Now. Here's Why.\",\n   \"publisher\": \"fool.com\",\n   \"publication_date\": \"2026-09-24\",\n   \"text\": \"Nvidia (NASDAQ: NVDA) has grown into the world's largest company, with a market cap of more than $5 trillion. Its graphics processing units (GPUs) are the gold standard for training and running high-level computing and artificial intelligence programs.\\nSome people may, however, look at Nvidia's stock price and think that the shine is coming off Jensen Huang's company. Shares are up 21% so far this year, which is fine, but that's not even close to the 239% gain in 2023, the 171% jump in 2024, or even last year's 39% increase. Meanwhile, stocks like Sandisk , Micron , and Intel are all up more than 200% this year and have become Wall Street darlings.\\nAre Nvidia's best days in the rearview mirror? Absolutely not. While the chipmaker may not replicate its massive 2023 gain, Nvidia remains an incredibly profitable company whose growth story is far from over. In fact, I think Nvidia is going to be putting up some wild numbers in the next two or three years, and that's why I'll be buying more Nvidia shares .\\nNvidia is still growing fast, despite its size\\nWhat stands out in Nvidia's most recent earnings report is that the company is still growing at a triple-digit rate. Revenue in the second quarter of fiscal 2027 (ending July 26, 2026) was $96.2 billion, up 106% from a year ago. Of that, $89 billion, or roughly 92.5% of the company's total revenue, came from its data center segment.\\nNet income was $59.68 billion, up 126% from a year ago, and earnings per share of $2.46 were up 128% from last year. Nvidia is very profitable -- its operating expenses totaled only $8.4 billion for the quarter, and its gross margin was an impressive 75%.\\n\\\"We're the only company in the world that creates and builds, offers an entire AI factory platform, a full-stack system. And customers can still mix and match,\\\" CEO Jensen Huang said. \\\"However, most companies just don't have the skills to do that or desire to do that. And so, there's an entire part of the market that we experience growth. There's sovereign AI, there're regional AIs, there're neoclouds, there're AI start-ups, there're enterprises, where we're seeing -- which represents about half of our business, and that's growing 100% a year.\\\"\\nNvidia's new product line is just getting started\\nNvidia's Hopper and Blackwell architectures have been enormously popular among hyperscalers and neoclouds building computing capacity to meet the growing demand for AI. But the company's Vera Rubin architecture, which uses NVLink chip-to-chip interconnects so its Rubin GPUs and Vera central processing units (CPUs) can share memory and improve efficiency, generates even more revenue. CFO Colette Kress said the Vera Rubin -- now in full production -- and full-stack AI platform has expanded the company's total addressable market from a range of $18 billion to $25 billion per gigawatt from Blackwell to $40 billion from Vera Rubin.\\nThat's one of the reasons why Huang has been so confident in his prediction -- which he made last year and recently reiterated -- that AI infrastructure spending would reach $3 trillion to $4 trillion by 2030. And speaking to reporters last week while in Scotland, Huang suggested that Nvidia would double its chip sales in the coming year.\\nIt's hard to get a group of people to agree on anything -- that's just human nature -- but Wall Street experts are nearly unanimous on Nvidia stock. Analysts at Rosenblatt have a \\\"Buy\\\" recommendation and a $390 price target, representing potential upside of 73%. Bernstein's analysts have a $400 price target and an \\\"Outperform\\\" rating, and Raymond James analysts have a whopping $515 price target and a \\\"Strong Buy\\\" rating.\\nNvidia is expected to report another strong quarter when it next reports earnings (likely in late November), with the consensus revenue estimate of $109.0 billion, up 91.2% from the previous year. And more of the same is expected for next year, following the Vera Rubin ramp-up.\"\n  },\n  {\n   \"article\": 10,\n   \"title\": \"When Should You Buy NVIDIA Stock After This Run?\",\n   \"publisher\": \"trefis.com\",\n   \"publication_date\": \"2026-09-28\",\n   \"text\": \"When Should You Buy NVIDIA Stock After This Run?\\nA $10,000 holding in NVIDIA (NVDA) stock three months ago was worth about $11,700 as of September 25, 2026. Yet against profits, the shares still trade near the low end of their ten-year range. If you have been waiting to buy NVIDIA, have you already missed it?\\nHave You Missed The Low Multiple On NVIDIA Stock?\\nNot if you judge it by earnings against its own past. The price-to-earnings ratio, or P/E, is the share price divided by a year of profit per share. NVIDIA trades at 28.1 times earnings , above the 22.1 for the S&P 500. Over the past ten years its P/E has ranged from 19.6 to 143.1, and today it is near the bottom.\\nBut a low P/E only stays low if profit holds up. NVIDIA’s operating margin, the share of revenue left after running costs, is 65%, its highest in ten years. Its three-year average is 59%. If margins slipped back toward that average, the same price would buy less profit.\\nCash looks less comfortable than earnings. Against operating cash flow, the cash the business brings in from running itself, the stock costs 40.4 times. The S&P 500 costs 14.5 times. Part of the cash has gone into inventory. On the fiscal Q2 2027 call, management said inventory rose to $32 billion as NVIDIA prepared for the Vera Rubin launch. On earnings or on cash, a price built on peak margins needs profit growth to continue.\\nHow Much Revenue Is NVIDIA Stock Counting On?\\nThe price appears to assume more revenue than NVIDIA itself has forecast for fiscal Q3 2027. Management guided revenue for that quarter to $108 billion, plus or minus 2%. The consensus forecast for that quarter is $111.3 billion, above the top of that range.\\nThat expectation has a record behind it. Management said fiscal Q2 2027 revenue of $96 billion more than doubled from a year earlier. Growth also sped up for the fourth quarter in a row.\\nThe newly launched Vera Rubin platform is one source of that revenue. Management expects it to make up about 20% of data center revenue in fiscal Q3 2027. Management says supply, not demand, is the limit. Management expects supply to remain a bottleneck at least through the end of fiscal 2028.\\nGross margin, the share of revenue left after making the product, is a different measure from operating margin. For fiscal Q3 2027 it is guided to 74%, plus or minus half a point, down from 75% in fiscal Q2 2027. Management pointed to higher component costs, especially memory. The next report has to beat NVIDIA’s own revenue forecast. The stock has not always taken its reports well.\\nNVIDIA’s Fiscal Q3 2027 Results Land in November\\nNVIDIA is expected to report on or around November 17, 2026. That report covers fiscal Q3 2027, and it will show whether revenue matches what the price appears to assume.\\nThe stock’s record after reports runs both ways. Over the last six reports, NVIDIA shares fell after four and rose after two, measured over two trading days. The biggest drop was 9.4%, after the February 25, 2026, report. The biggest gain was 3.8%, after the August 26, 2026, report.\\nA broad market sell-off is a separate risk. During the 2025 tariff shock, NVIDIA stock fell 32% from peak to low, against 19% for the S&P 500.\\nIf fiscal Q3 2027 revenue lands below $111.3 billion, the price will have asked for more than NVIDIA delivered. Buying now would then prove early. Suppose revenue clears $111.3 billion and gross margin comes in at 74% or better. The business will then have kept pace with the run. Buying at today’s price would not have been too late.\"\n  }\n ],\n \"answers\": [\n  {\n   \"answer\": \"B43\",\n   \"article\": 10,\n   \"figures\": [\n    {\n     \"statement\": \"e1\",\n     \"n\": 1,\n     \"measure\": \"revenue guidance\",\n     \"period\": \"fiscal Q3 2027\",\n     \"fixed_period\": \"FY2027-Q3\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"statement\": \"e2\",\n     \"n\": 1,\n     \"measure\": \"revenue consensus forecast\",\n     \"period\": \"fiscal Q3 2027\",\n     \"fixed_period\": \"FY2027-Q3\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"statement\": \"e3\",\n     \"n\": 1,\n     \"measure\": \"revenue\",\n     \"period\": \"fiscal Q2 2027\",\n     \"fixed_period\": \"FY2027-Q2\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"statement\": \"e4\",\n     \"n\": 1,\n     \"measure\": \"inventory\",\n     \"period\": \"fiscal Q2 2027\",\n     \"fixed_period\": \"FY2027-Q2\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"statement\": \"e5\",\n     \"n\": 1,\n     \"measure\": \"Vera Rubin share of data center revenue\",\n     \"period\": \"fiscal Q3 2027\",\n     \"fixed_period\": \"FY2027-Q3\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"statement\": \"e7\",\n     \"n\": 1,\n     \"measure\": \"gross margin guidance\",\n     \"period\": \"fiscal Q3 2027\",\n     \"fixed_period\": \"FY2027-Q3\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"statement\": \"e7\",\n     \"n\": 2,\n     \"measure\": \"gross margin\",\n     \"period\": \"fiscal Q2 2027\",\n     \"fixed_period\": \"FY2027-Q2\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"statement\": \"e8\",\n     \"n\": 1,\n     \"measure\": \"operating margin\",\n     \"period\": null,\n     \"fixed_period\": null\n    },\n    {\n     \"statement\": \"e8\",\n     \"n\": 2,\n     \"measure\": \"operating margin, three-year average\",\n     \"period\": \"three-year average\",\n     \"fixed_period\": null\n    }\n   ]\n  },\n  {\n   \"answer\": \"B45\",\n   \"article\": 3,\n   \"figures\": []\n  },\n  {\n   \"answer\": \"B48\",\n   \"article\": 5,\n   \"figures\": [\n    {\n     \"statement\": \"e1\",\n     \"n\": 1,\n     \"measure\": \"revenue\",\n     \"period\": \"fiscal second quarter\",\n     \"fixed_period\": null\n    },\n    {\n     \"statement\": \"e1\",\n     \"n\": 2,\n     \"measure\": \"revenue growth vs. same period last year\",\n     \"period\": \"fiscal second quarter\",\n     \"fixed_period\": null\n    },\n    {\n     \"statement\": \"e1\",\n     \"n\": 3,\n     \"measure\": \"gross margin\",\n     \"period\": \"fiscal second quarter\",\n     \"fixed_period\": null\n    },\n    {\n     \"statement\": \"e2\",\n     \"n\": 1,\n     \"measure\": \"revenue growth\",\n     \"period\": \"next fiscal year\",\n     \"fixed_period\": null\n    },\n    {\n     \"st